<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guba, Kuba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quba is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>, near the Caspian coast. In the 18th century it was the seat of the Quba Khanate, whose ruler Fatali Khan united much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied it in 1806. Across the river lies Krasnaya Sloboda, one of the largest Mountain Jewish settlements in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quba.jpg" alt="Quba" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gulustan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quba_385.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
